m we're motivated intrinsically as much  as extrinsically  um uh and it became obvious in the mid  20th century that you know rats  were willing to walk across an  electrified fence just to peek around  the corner  uh monkeys are as willing to lever press  for uh  to look out a window as they are for  food um and so this  started an effort within psychology to  try to understand the nature of  intrinsic as opposed to extrinsic  motivation  and there's a long and wonderful history  here  the computer science story i think is  people like google brains mark bellamar  who's working on uh extending  count-based exploration  into non-tabular settings um  people like jurgen schmidhumer who's  thinking about intrinsic motivation as  the ability to compress uh information  people at open ai  like yuri berda and harry edwards um  and here at berkeley uh people like  deepak pathik  pulkit agrawal um trevor daryl  exploring intrinsic motivation uh based  on on the idea  that the agent be motivated to take  actions essentially which surprise it  um so as this formal uh work in rl is  getting worked out there's this  all of these connections to infant  psychology um there was a great story  that  alison gotnik berkeley psychologist told  me  about reading about uh trevor  and some of his students work in the  berkeley newsletter and they were  talking about how interested they were  in taking her  ideas about infant uh curiosity and  applying them to rl  and she emailed them like guys i'm i'm  right here i'm like  across the street uh let's actually  collaborate on this and so it's been  really exciting to see  um those two worlds come together um  and on the one hand developmental uh  psychologists are using  rl as a formal model to explain infant  behavior and at the same time  uh the people in rl are turning to uh  what we know about infants to think  about  uh motivation and intrinsic drive uh  that might be useful  uh just in in exploration uh for rl  so the third part of the book um  gets most squarely into the question of  normativity and aligning deep rl agents  with human norms and human values so  one of the things one of the central  themes that anyone knows  um in rls what's called imitation  learning or sometimes behavior cloning  um and there's a really really rich  story here  um not only the computer science story  um in the book we  we meet dean palmerlow from cmu who um  is crazy enough to drive all the way  from pittsburgh to lake erie on the  highway for two hours  um letting a neural network steer his  car in 1990  um using a system that had one tenth of  the processing power  of a first generation apple watch um  so the the surprisingly long uh and  slightly daredevil  history of behavior cloning uh in  self-driving cars that continues all  all the way to this day and we meet  waymo engineer  stefan ross who developed the dagger  algorithm for avoiding cascading errors  um  in imitation learning there's also this  wonderful human story here too  which is that um zoologically all of our  words for imitation not just in english  but in  almost every language uh we say you know  to ape something  uh but the real prolific imitator in  nature  is not apes at all and there's an entire  uh  primatology literature on just on that  topic  um but it is in fact uh humans um  and furthermore the human capacity for  imitation is extremely  sophisticated um and goes way beyond  merely duplicating uh behavior in in  ways that are  surprising um and and really i think  informative uh for thinking about how  this might work um  with machines um so  uh there's also i think a really  interesting connection  in imitation um to  uh not just primatology but  philosophical ethics um  and in the interest of time i won't get  too deeply into it but there's a there's  a classic  philosophical tension going back to the  1970s between what are called  possibilism and actualism so  do you do the very best thing possible  in a situation even if it requires  a very precise follow-through and you  know that you'll screw it up  um or do you do um the the lesser action  that you know you can actually follow  through  so this debate has now been going on for  uh something like  40 years and it's absolutely relevant to  thinking about  things like batch off policy rl um so  i'm really intrigued in the way that  those  literatures are starting to come into  contact  um in chapter eight we get into  arguably the heart of contemporary ai  safety research so ideas around  inverse reinforcement learning  cooperative inverse reinforcement  learning  deep rl from human preferences and so  forth um  and irl itself has this wonderfully  colorful history that it really goes  back to  uh stuart russell walking to safeway um  and thinking about his gait as he went  down the hill  um and this gets him thinking about you  know what  what is it that animal and human gates  optimize  why is it the case that it we still need  to hire motion capture people and we  can't reliably produce like realistic  looking gates  um and uh this gets into the idea of irl  so if the human gate is the answer  what's the question um there's an entire  interdisciplinary literature here  just on the science of gait and all the  different theories that people have had  over many decades for  why horse gates are certain ways and why  there are phase  transitions in quadruped gates at  certain speeds and  are they optimizing stress on the joints  are they optimizing you know  calorie load etc and  irl now it directly offers us a way to  answer and address questions like that  um so there's this purely  theoretical story that goes through um  you know peter abel's work on getting  helicopters to do  stunts uh people like chelsea finn  working on things like  guided cost learning um to uh  jan laika and paul cristiano very  memorably teaching  a uh a mujoko agent to do a backflip  just  by comparing different clips  and picking the one that looks slightly  more like a backflip um  there there are i think many  interdisciplinary questions  here um there are ethical questions  about recommendation systems you know  you can you can infer  what someone wants but um are they  acting the way that they want to be  acting in the first place i think that's  a that's an interesting question  um more broadly as robots become more  capable  and able to work sort of our elbow to  elbow with humans  in manufacturing settings and so forth  um  there's an entire interdisciplinary  literature on human  teamwork um and so i'm thinking about  people like  mit roboticist julie shaw who has done  a lot of work borrowing ideas straight  out of the human human teaming  literature  and showing that they apply um basically  wholesale  uh into thinking about human robot uh  collaboration uh in in factory settings  and so i think there's so many cases  like this  where there is just this windfall of  insights  ready ready to be plucked basically um  and as these systems get more and more  capable i think that's only going to  continue to be the case so the book ends  with a chapter about uncertainty  um so we meet stanislav petrov the  soviet officer who uh single-handedly  saved 100 million people's lives  by not doing anything when his missile  system told him that the u.s was  attacking  but the attack seemed weird to him  so he simply didn't do anything um and  uh  you know to some degree to save the  world by that um so this theme of  uncertainty and in particular what  action to take  in the face of uncertainty i think is  very interesting uh in an ml context  so you have people like oregon's tom  dietrich  who talks about what he calls the open  category problem you know it's one thing  to classify uh classify images as a set  of  one of a set of categories but most of  the things in the world are actually in  none of those categories  so how do how do you deal with that um  there are people like oxford yaringall  who are working on the uncertainty  estimates you get out of bayesian neural  networks and how you can approximate  that using dropout  um there are people here at berkeley and  chai  um smith and millie dylan hadfield  mannell peter  uh abeel anka dragon stuart russell  working on the idea that if you're  interested in obedience in a system  the ability to intervene and stop it  the system must necessarily be uncertain  have some uncertainty over what it  thinks your objectives are  again here at berkeley gregory khan and  his colleagues at bear have worked on  robots that slow down  when they um their collision predictor  model becomes uncertain  um using a mixture of dropout and other  techniques  and i think there are there are a number  of questions here um  in purely in the theoretical computer  science side  around how do you measure uncertainty  how do you measure  the impact that your action might have  um and then what do you do in the face  of that uncertainty and with that sense  of  impact so on the impact side there are  people like future of humanity institute  stuart armstrong  deep minds victoria krakovana oregon's  alex turner  uh working on operationalizing uh this  notion of high impact actions in an rl  setting  um and uh there's also an entire medical  ethics literature  on what do you do in the face of  uncertainty there's also a legal  literature on this idea of preliminary  injunctions or irreparable harm  and i think all of these things start to  become relevant and start to feed into  what we  are doing in the field of rl so  to conclude i think as we  gather here at the end of 2020 um  in sum i think we're ready to to etch  another chapter into this history so  we have seen first the promise and  secondly the peril  uh of these systems and we're now at the  beginning of what i see as the the third  phase  um there's a small but brilliant group  of people  beginning to rally and muster around  these images  these issues the first responders as i  say are on the scene  and i hope we can grow these numbers and  i hope that um  the book inspires people into this area  which i see as  being not only one of the most  fascinating and dynamic  um areas but also one of the most  important projects um  in computer science and and frankly in  all of science um  and i think this is a challenge for me  this is an opportunity to meet that  challenge head on  and also a chance to learn i think  something really  radical and profound about ourselves so  as a place to end i i was going through  some archival papers of alan turing's  and i came across a conversation that he  had on bbc radio in 1952  and um you know we'll skip some of the  context but he's saying you know i was  doing a lot of work trying to teach this  machine to do something very simple  and a very great deal of instruction was  needed before i could get into  any results um the machine learned so  slowly that it needed a great deal of  teaching  and one of his uh fellow panelists  interrupts him and says  but who is learning you or the machine  and he says well i suppose we both were  so with that i just want to say thank  you and i welcome any questions that you  might have so thank you very much  thanks very much brian that was uh  really great  um you uh are welcome to use the q a  feature if you'd like to um  uh ask ask any questions um well  while folks are thinking about it um uh  so it struck me in looking at the the  table of contents of your book that you  went through it was a very uh  logical progression you know with the  titles that you had it could have been a  textbook but  um it's really clear that the book's  filled with um  you know fascinating stories um so i'm  curious about so  for instance i was surprised to learn  about um walter pitts being a a homeless  prodigy um yes but  so i i guess um i'm interested in how  the  the stories have shaped how you chose to  present the topics in the  in the book yeah i think that you know  one of the  challenges in writing non-fiction is  that you have to juggle these different  things  right so to your point it is to some  degree a kind of a textbook it's sort of  a  sheep and uh wolf in sheep's clothing  it's you know  a textbook um that is uh presented as a  as a narrative um so that it can reach a  broader audience and  so for me i had to really juggle the  question of what i saw  as being kind of the central result in  an area  with what seemed like the best story  um and sometimes those things lined up  really naturally  um so for example you know rich caruana  discovering that  pneumonia uh his the pneumonia model  predicts that if you have asthma  that you should go home and you know  it'll be fine um  that is both a wonderful story on its  own  and it also happens that rich is today  you know one of the people who's really  active in this area so  that that kind of presented itself to me  on a platter  um other cases where um you know in the  uncertainty chapter  it opens with this somewhat canonical  story of the soviet uh  officer um and  uh that's not it's not on its face a  machine learning story uh  story although he was using a machine  learning system that was  giving him this uh assessment that okay  it looks like there's the u.s attack  we're raiding it high confidence um  uh and he knows that what he's supposed  to do is pick up the phone and  tell his superiors but he knows that  they're going to order this missile  launch  and so he's effectively it's effectively  his hand on the button and he  decides not to do anything um and so  there was just a  an anecdote that i found of a machine  learning researcher  um talking about  in the past when if someone had given  them a thought experiment where there  was a button they could press that would  convert the universe to hedonium  you know computationally optimized  matter for producing unpleasant  experiences  they would have pressed that button and  now they're not so  sure uh you know and when people mention  that thought experiment they say i don't  know maybe  maybe we shouldn't press that button and  so that for me was both  um a literary moment where i could  connect this historical example to a  present-day contemporary  researcher but also a chance to have  some literary symmetry where the chapter  begins with someone not pushing a button  and it ends with someone not pushing a  button so um  each chapter offered me different  opportunities for that but it was  very much um an exercise to try to  balance the the curriculum if you will  and the just the the pure storytelling  yeah fantastic um we  we have a few questions but we're  running short on time maybe  maybe just the first one up there anna  asks are there any particular areas that  you think should be receiving more  attention  than they are now or any communities  that you think that you think should be  talking to each other more  oh it's it's hard to say in a way  because i feel like the entire book is  that you know  it's like all of these people should be  talking and for me  one of the one of the pleasures of the  book um  is that i actually um you know i got to  be more than a fly on the wall i  actually got to  um have conversations with people where  i said oh  you know so-and-so is working on exactly  that and you know here's someone in  cambridge who's working on that  um and people's ears would perk up and i  get to write letters of introduction and  so  um you know for me that's one of the  great pleasures of getting to do  a sort of inter broad interdisciplinary  work like this  is to to actually get to stimulate some  of those connections  um it's it's really hard to pick just  one but i mean i think  that a lot of the work on infant  cognition is is pretty amazing and that  comes up again and again it comes up in  shaping because infants are really good  at thwarting i mean and small children  not just infants  really good at thwarting the incentives  that you try to design as a parent  in ways that are i think indicative of  problems that we can expect from  you know rl systems more generally  all the way through intrinsic motivation  to imitation to  the ability of children as young as  18 months old to infer from your actions  what you're trying to do even if you're  failing to do it  which is this sort of irl thing so i  think that  the connections between the infant mind  and and  ai obviously go all the way back to the  very beginning turing was talking about  you know a man  imagine building a program that  simulates the child's mind not not the  adult's mind then all we have to do is  subject it to the appropriate course of  education so that's a very old idea  um but in some ways it feels like it's  just bearing fruit now  and and it has far is far from being  exhausted so i think that's that's  really a really rich area that has a lot  to offer  all right well i think we're going to  have to cut it off there there is  several more questions but uh we're out  of time  thanks again ryan that was a really uh  fascinating talk  it is my pleasure absolutely thank you
 

